## 5. The three named invariants — how each is made violable

The goal names three invariant violations that fixtures must exercise. Each is encoded with
**redundant, independent** structural defences, so that a single relaxed keyword during future
maintenance does not silently disarm the invariant.

### 5.1 Authored confidence

| Defence | Encoding | Defeated fixture |
|---|---|---|
| D1 | `confidence` is `{"type":"object"}` — a bare number is a type error | `I01` |
| D2 | `confidence.method` closed enum with no `authored`/`asserted` member | `I02` |
| D3 | `confidence.inputs` `minItems: 1` — a derivation with no inputs *is* authoring | `I03` |
| D4 | `confidence.computed_by` required, pattern `^sakg\.confidence\.[a-z_]+$` — a component, not a person | `I21` |

D3 is the subtle one and the reason the invariant is stated as *authored*, not *hand-written*:
`{"value": 0.95, "method": "evidence_weighted", "inputs": []}` is syntactically a derivation and
semantically an assertion. Requiring ≥1 input closes it.

### 5.2 Range, so `value` cannot be smuggled

`confidence.value` is `{"type":"number","minimum":0,"maximum":1}`. `I04` uses `1.4`. This is not a
named goal invariant, but an out-of-range confidence is the cheapest way to fake certainty and costs
one fixture.

### 5.3 Frequency-based promotion

| Defence | Encoding | Defeated fixture |
|---|---|---|
| D1 | `promotion.basis` closed enum of 8 epistemic bases; no count-shaped member | `I05` |
| D2 | `promotion` has `unevaluatedProperties: false` — `observation_count`, `occurrences`, `seen_n_times` are all rejected as unknown | `I06` |
| D3 | `promotion.evidence_refs` `minItems: 1` — a promotion justified by a tally carries no evidence refs | `I06`, `I07` |
| D4 | basis↔transition binding (§4.4) — even a legal basis is rejected on the wrong edge | `I17` |

D1 and D2 are deliberately independent: D1 catches the honest spelling (`basis: "frequency"`), D2
catches the dishonest one (a legitimate-looking basis with a counter riding alongside). An
implementer who "fixes" a validation failure by adding `observation_count` to the allowed properties
still trips D1 if they name it, and still trips D3 if they have no evidence.

### 5.4 Deleted node

| Defence | Encoding | Defeated fixture |
|---|---|---|
| D1 | `status` closed enum — no `DELETED` member | `I08` |
| D2 | top-level `unevaluatedProperties: false` — no `deleted`, `tombstoned`, `removed` flag | `I09` |
| D3 | `if status == RETRACTED then required: ["retraction"]` — erasure without a tombstone fails | `I10` |
| D4 | `RETRACTED` absent from every `from` position — no resurrection | `I11` |
| D5 | detail-block exclusivity: a non-`RETRACTED` node carrying a `retraction` block fails | `I13` |

D5 deserves a note. Without it, a producer could half-delete a node — mark it `ACTIVE` for readers
while attaching a retraction that some other consumer honours. Requiring that exactly the
status-matching detail block be present makes the lifecycle state single-valued and readable from
one field.

